%\clearpage
\begin{theorem}[Swapping Identity]
In the limit where measurements vanish, the swapping identity holds:
		%
\begin{equation}
	\equality
\label{eq:main}
\end{equation}
		%
\label{thm:swapping-identity}
\end{theorem}
   %%%   %%%   %%%   %%%   %%%   %%%   %%%   %%%
\begin{proof}
When the merit function in \eqref{eq:phi-star}  achieves the smallest possible value $\phi_{\star}(b) = 0$ we have
		%
\begin{equation*}
	\er - \frac{\inner{t^{b}, R}^{2}} {\etb} = 0.
%\label{eq:}
\end{equation*}
		%
Equation \eqref{eq:swap} follows immediately.

Inequality \ref{eq:main} is sharp, with equality achieved in the perfect data limit $\normt{R_{\mathcal{N}}}\to 0$, providing the swapping identity
		%
\begin{equation}
	\swap
\label{eq:main}
\end{equation}
		%
\end{proof}


